<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Sikh Misls</h1><p class="st-meta">1748 CE – 1799 CE (approx.) · capital Amritsar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A confederacy of Sikh warrior bands that took control of the Punjab from the Mughals and Afghans in the 18th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the execution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banda-singh-bahadur">Banda Singh Bahadur</a> in 1716, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> fighters organised in bands during decades of persecution by Mughal governors and invasions by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a>. In 1748, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a>, they combined into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-khalsa">Dal Khalsa</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jassa-singh-ahluwalia">Jassa Singh Ahluwalia</a>, grouped into eleven or twelve misls, each led by a sardar.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The misls were largely autonomous units that shared conquered land and met at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarbat-khalsa">Sarbat Khalsa</a> assemblies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a>, where collective decisions, gurmatta, were taken. They collected rakhi, a protection levy, from villages. Despite the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a> massacre of 1762, they took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> in 1765 and rebuilt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Harmandir Sahib</a>. Leading misls included the Bhangi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahluwalia-misl">Ahluwalia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramgarhia-misl">Ramgarhia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukerchakia-misl">Sukerchakia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Rivalries among the misls grew after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a> threat faded. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>, head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukerchakia-misl">Sukerchakia misl</a>, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> in 1799 and absorbed the other misls into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>, of which he was proclaimed maharaja in 1801.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sikh-misls.jpg" alt="Sikh Misls" width="320" height="299"><figcaption>Image: Borders by Rawn3012 using Gimp · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Sikh_Confederacy.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1748 CE – 1799 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Amritsar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jassa Singh Ahluwalia, Jassa Singh Ramgarhia, Charat Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sikhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Punjabi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India, Pakistan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Misldars, Sikh Confederacy, Dal Khalsa</dd></dl></aside></div>
